# Multimodule Configuration

From version 2.12 of this Plugin, you can [inline](./inline-checker-config.html) the configuration directly in the parent POM.

You can configure the Checkstyle Plugin for large multimodule projects.
It requires some setup.

This example uses a sample project called _whizbang_.
The project has the structure below:

```unknown
whizbang
|-- pom.xml
|-- core
|   `-- pom.xml
|-- gui
|   `-- pom.xml
|-- jmx
|   `-- pom.xml
`-- src
```

Create a subproject to house the resources
------------------------------------------

Create another subproject that houses your common configuration.
Call it _build-tools_.
Put the resources that you want to include in the subproject.
In this example, add configuration files for the Checkstyle Plugin.
You can also include configuration files for other plugins, like the PMD plugin, in the same subproject.

```unknown
whizbang
|-- pom.xml
|-- build-tools
|   |-- src
|   |   `-- main
|   |       `-- resources
|   |           `-- whizbang
|   |               |-- checkstyle.xml
|   |               `-- LICENSE.TXT
|   `-- pom.xml
|-- core
|-- gui
|-- jmx
`-- src
```

**Tip:** Put the resources into a unique subdirectory so that they do not conflict with anyone else.

The `pom.xml` file for _build-tools_ looks like this:

```xml
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <groupId>com.example.whizbang</groupId>
  <artifactId>build-tools</artifactId>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <name>Build Tools</name>
</project>
```

Configure the other projects to use it
--------------------------------------

Now include the Checkstyle configuration in the top level `pom.xml`.

**Note:** You must specify a plugin dependency on `build-tools` in the `<build>` element of your `pom.xml`.
This dependency will not work in the `<reporting>` element.
The `<reporting>` element does not support plugin dependencies.
Do the rest of the configuration in the normal way in the `<reporting>` element.

```xml
<project>
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <groupId>com.example.whizbang</groupId>
  <artifactId>whizbang-parent</artifactId>
  <version>1.0</version>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>WhizBang Parent</name>
  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <dependencies>
          <dependency>
            <groupId>com.example.whizbang</groupId>
            <artifactId>build-tools</artifactId>
            <version>1.0</version>
          </dependency>
        </dependencies>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
        <configuration>
          <configLocation>whizbang/checkstyle.xml</configLocation>
          <headerLocation>whizbang/LICENSE.txt</headerLocation>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
  <modules>
    <module>build-tools</module>
    <module>core</module>
    <module>jmx</module>
    <module>gui</module>
  </modules>
</project>
```

Make sure that you do not include the Maven Checkstyle Plugin in your submodules.
A submodule definition overrides the top level parent POM definition.

The plugin resolves the values of `configLocation` and `headerLocation` from the classpath.
The _build-tools_ JAR is in the classpath.
You declared the JAR as a dependency to the plugin.

Then run a build of the site:

```shell
mvn site
```

Every subproject now uses the same Checkstyle setup and configuration.
